The following non-normative example shows the intended JSON-first shape for a derived topic view.

```json
{
  "name": "tls-topic",
  "description": "Select requirements related to TLS behavior in QUIC.",
  "match": {
    "all": [
      {
        "any": [
          {
            "literal": {
              "fields": [
                "requirement.title",
                "requirement.statement",
                "requirement.notes",
                "requirement.trace.upstream_refs"
              ],
              "value": "TLS",
              "case": "insensitive"
            }
          },
          {
            "regex": {
              "fields": [
                "requirement.statement",
                "requirement.notes",
                "requirement.trace.upstream_refs"
              ],
              "pattern": "\\b(handshake|certificate|resumption|0-RTT|key schedule)\\b",
              "case": "insensitive"
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "not": {
          "requirement_ids": {
            "values": [
              "REQ-QUIC-LOSS-0017"
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  "include_requirements": [
    "REQ-QUIC-TLS-0001"
  ],
  "exclude_requirements": [
    "REQ-QUIC-LOSS-0017"
  ]
}
```

Tooling may expose other authoring syntaxes, but the canonical interchange form remains a JSON object with explicit operators and fields.
